:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048433}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403151}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"} :step/id #uuid "d619a409-5110-5f14-981a-e1e96235dd25" :step/math-academy-id 13173 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060547 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059686 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059514 :question/id #uuid "ede68192-5d89-5204-8c49-a1eeebfbf48c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3842"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a119150-6d23-59bd-90c9-699de3a75795" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049407}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234166}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"} :step/id #uuid "80b57f6b-0f3a-58c6-a53f-22f4b5c50a44" :step/math-academy-id 13174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060550}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060548 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060901 :tutorial/content "All the angles we have constructed so far have been inside the range $(-360^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. However, we can also construct angles outside this range.\n\nThe way we do this involves **coterminal angles**. Coterminal angles are different angles that appear as the same angle in the coordinate plane. For example, the angles $0^\\circ$ and $360^\\circ$ are coterminal because a rotation of $360^\\circ$ is the same as the rotation of $0^\\circ$. In general, whenever we add or subtract a multiple of $360^\\circ$, we get a coterminal angle.\n\nThe standard way in which we draw a **positive** angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane is as follows:\n\n- Subtract integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, to represent the angle $405^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane, we first subtract $360^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n405^{∘} - 360^{∘} = 45^{∘}✓\n$$\n\nThe result, $45^\\circ$, is a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$. So, to draw the angle $405^\\circ$, we draw the coterminal angle $45^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/6c/6c96b1f8b34764c0d1a8ffb2362349d3ac3f4f99eaaf5c8e4c249fab1f29ceb3.png)\n\n**Note:** For every multiple of $\\color{blue}360^\\circ$ we subtract, we make one complete rotation counter-clockwise around the origin. A rotation of ${\\color{blue}360^\\circ}$ is the same as the rotation of ${\\color{blue}0^\\circ}$, so it doesn't contribute to our final angle.\n\nHere, we subtracted ${\\color{blue}360^\\circ}$ a single time, so we make a single rotation.\n\n![](images/dd/dd44ea334cc1cf709685a71a5a88025955e5cc1fb96cd9d938272b8024b99acc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c50e7f7-6a06-51b1-8970-2f34f25890c7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2709 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "36e80ef2-d5c8-59b3-8dde-d93df12c37e0" :step/math-academy-id 13181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060547}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060902 :tutorial/content "To find a coterminal angle of a given angle $\\theta$, we rotate $360^\\circ$ clockwise or counter-clockwise from $\\theta$. We could do this again and again, each time looping by $360^\\circ$ to find an infinite number of coterminal angles:\n$\\theta \\pm 360^\\circ n,\\quad \\text{where} n \\text{is any integer.}$\n\nFor instance, the angles $-240^\\circ$, $120^\\circ$, $480^\\circ$, and $840^\\circ$ are all coterminal.\n\n![](images/16/1684bdb452645ca612d0e0b08321b688f8d3e4a7a6de005a3afd620707eae9ee.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a87549c4-ee2e-552b-95ea-50727cc87fbe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2710 :tutorial/title "Finding Multiple Coterminal Angles"} :step/id #uuid "a1c2e014-ae34-535b-8a47-0683fdb921ab" :step/math-academy-id 13182 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060544}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060550 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060903 :tutorial/content "The method for constructing a *negative* angle $\\theta$ outside the range $(-360^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$ is similar to the method for positive angles, but with one main difference: we *add* multiples of $360^\\circ$, instead of subtracting. So the steps are:\n\n- Add integer multiples of $360^\\circ$ until we get a coterminal angle that lies in the range $[0^\\circ, 360^\\circ)$.\n- Draw the resulting angle in the coordinate plane.\n\nFor example, we can represent the angle $-660^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-660^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= -300^{∘} \\\\\n- 300^{∘} + 360^{∘} &= 60^{∘}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we draw the angle $60^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/e7/e76aff5a8e7bc805be56a61db114f111a9ab15f54b01eb3dd5542c1023224b85.png)\n\nAgain, for every multiple of $360^\\circ$ that we add, we make one complete rotation around the origin clockwise. A rotation of ${\\color{blue}360^\\circ}$ is the same as the rotation of ${\\color{blue}0^\\circ}$, so it doesn't contribute to our final angle." :tutorial/id #uuid "b9973405-ead1-5dfa-92ff-238e697e4ad2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2712 :tutorial/title "Constructing Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"} :step/id #uuid "2b4977be-02bb-5aef-85fd-9b94974a8ea1" :step/math-academy-id 13195 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060546}}] :activity/title "Coterminal Angles" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059936}]}
